{"y":1999,"label":"1998–99","coach":"Jim Calhoun","conf":"Big East","confShort":"Big East","w":34,"l":2,"cw":16,"cl":2,"confFinish":"1st in Big East","finish":"champ","seed":1,"region":"West","apPre":2,"apHigh":1,"apFinal":3,"srs":24.73,"sos":8.79,"ortg":109.4,"drtg":86.8,"pace":70.4,"ppg":77.2,"oppg":61.3,"story":{"headline":"We Shocked the World","text":"Rip Hamilton passed on the NBA, the entire starting five returned, and UConn started 19–0, holding the No. 1 ranking for a program-record 10 weeks. The first loss came February 1 to Syracuse in Hartford with Hamilton and center Jake Voskuhl injured, and the Huskies answered by winning 70–59 at No. 4 Stanford. They won the Big East regular season and beat St. John's 82–63 for the conference tournament title, with Kevin Freeman named MVP. As the No. 1 seed in the West, UConn beat UTSA, New Mexico and Iowa, then Gonzaga 67–62 for the program's first Final Four. After a 64–58 semifinal win over Ohio State in St. Petersburg, roughly nine-point underdogs UConn beat top-ranked Duke 77–74 on March 29, 1999. Hamilton scored 27 and was named Most Outstanding Player, El-Amin scored UConn's final four points, Ricky Moore hounded Duke's guards — and as time expired El-Amin shouted into a TV camera: \"We shocked the world!\" The 34–2 record remains the best winning percentage in program history.","moments":[{"date":"1999-03-06","title":"Big East tournament Championship: W vs St. John's","text":"UConn beat St. John's 82–63 (Championship) at Madison Square Garden, New York."},{"date":"1999-03-11","title":"NCAA First Round: W vs UTSA","text":"UConn beat UTSA 91–66 in the NCAA tournament first round at McNichols Sports Arena, Denver, Colorado."},{"date":"1999-03-13","title":"NCAA Second Round: W vs New Mexico","text":"UConn beat New Mexico 78–56 in the NCAA tournament second round at McNichols Sports Arena, Denver, Colorado."},{"date":"1999-03-18","title":"NCAA Sweet Sixteen: W vs Iowa","text":"UConn beat Iowa 78–68 in the NCAA tournament sweet sixteen at America West Arena, Phoenix, Arizona."},{"date":"1999-03-20","title":"NCAA Elite Eight: W vs Gonzaga","text":"UConn beat Gonzaga 67–62 in the NCAA tournament elite eight at America West Arena, Phoenix, Arizona."},{"date":"1999-03-27","title":"NCAA Final Four: W vs Ohio State","text":"UConn beat Ohio State 64–58 in the NCAA tournament final four at Tropicana Field, St. Petersburg, Florida."},{"date":"1999-03-29","title":"\"We shocked the world!\"","text":"UConn beats Duke 77–74 in St. Petersburg for its first national championship; Richard Hamilton is Most Outstanding Player."}],"honors":["NCAA national champions","Big East tournament champions","Big East regular-season champions","Richard Hamilton – NCAA Final Four Most Outstanding Player","Richard Hamilton – Consensus first-team All-American","Richard Hamilton – Big East Co-Player of the Year","Kevin Freeman – Big East Tournament MVP","Richard Hamilton – NCAA Regional Most Outstanding Player","Richard Hamilton – First-team All-Big East"],"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1998%E2%80%9399_Connecticut_Huskies_men's_basketball_team","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UConn_Huskies_men%27s_basketball","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jim_Calhoun","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Hamilton_(basketball)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khalid_El-Amin","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ricky_Moore_(basketball)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kevin_Freeman_(basketball)"]},"roster":[{"pid":"richard-hamilton-1","name":"Richard Hamilton","num":"32","cls":"JR","pos":"G","ht":"6-6","wt":185,"home":"Coatesville, PA","photo":"assets/players/richard-hamilton-1.jpg?v=b516aeaf","photoWide":true,"pg":{"g":34,"gs":34,"mp":32.1,"pts":21.5,"trb":4.8,"ast":2.7,"stl":1.2,"blk":0.3,"tov":2.4,"fg_pct":0.443,"fg3_pct":0.347,"ft_pct":0.833,"orb":1.5,"pf":1.6},"tot":{"mp":1091,"fg":247,"fga":557,"fg3":68,"fg3a":196,"ft":170,"fta":204,"orb":52,"drb":111,"trb":163,"ast":91,"stl":40,"blk":9,"tov":82,"pf":56,"pts":732,"g